change:
  id: CHG-016
  title: Wire a real benchmark evidence source into cost_latency_budget
  status: delivered
  intent: Give EvolveRunner an injectable BenchmarkRunner and EvolveRunRequest a benchmark-budgets map, then have saaa-evolve compose a real JmhBenchmarkRunner behind new --benchmark/--benchmark-budget flags, so cost_latency_budget can discriminate an over-budget candidate from an in-budget one instead of always reporting 1.0. Default behaviour without those flags is unchanged.
  audience_level: developer
  relates_to:
    - Q-004
    - SYS-001
    - ARCH-001

deltas:
  - op: MODIFIED
    capability: mutation-fitness-loop
    requirement: EvolveRunner accepts an injected BenchmarkRunner and EvolveRunRequest carries a benchmark-budgets map threaded into ScoringConfig, so cost_latency_budget has a real evidence source when a caller supplies one; saaa-evolve composes this through new repeatable --benchmark and --benchmark-budget options backed by a real JmhBenchmarkRunner from :cli's new dependency on :benchmarks. With neither flag given, the run is unchanged: a constant empty benchmark list and an empty budget map, so the objective still starts and stays at 1.0.
    scenarios:
      - id: S1
        when: EvolveRunner is constructed with an injected BenchmarkRunner whose measured evidence is within a configured budget
        then: cost_latency_budget in the resulting FitnessResult is 1.0
      - id: S2
        when: EvolveRunner is constructed with an injected BenchmarkRunner whose measured evidence exceeds a configured budget
        then: cost_latency_budget is budget/measured, strictly below 1.0, and the aggregate score is strictly lower than an otherwise-identical in-budget run
        and:
          - this proves the objective can now discriminate one candidate from another, which it could not before this change
      - id: S3
        when: saaa-evolve runs with neither --benchmark nor --benchmark-budget given
        then: EvolveCommand still passes a constant empty benchmark list and an empty budget map, so cost_latency_budget stays at its 1.0 starting point and every prior README example is unaffected
      - id: S4
        when: saaa-evolve runs with --benchmark name=jmh-include-regex and a --benchmark-budget the real measurement cannot meet
        then: a real JmhBenchmarkRunner constructed from :benchmarks is invoked, its measured evidence lowers cost_latency_budget, and an otherwise-promoting candidate whose declared behaviour cases all pass is discarded because the weighted score falls below the 0.80 threshold
        and:
          - this exercises the new :cli dependency on :benchmarks end to end, beyond the injected-BenchmarkRunner wiring S1/S2 exercise directly

acceptance:
  - scenario: S1
    test_id: adapters/evolve/EvolveRunnerTest.discriminatesCandidatesByAConfiguredBenchmarkBudget
    layer: component
  - scenario: S2
    test_id: adapters/evolve/EvolveRunnerTest.discriminatesCandidatesByAConfiguredBenchmarkBudget
    layer: component
  - scenario: S3
    test_id: cli/EvolveCommandAcceptanceTest.runsOneGenerationWithFixtureProfileAndReportsADecision
    layer: acceptance
  - scenario: S4
    test_id: cli/EvolveCommandAcceptanceTest.wiresARealBenchmarkRunnerSoAnOverBudgetMeasurementDiscardsAnOtherwisePromotingCandidate
    layer: acceptance

tasks:
  - id: T1
    slice: add a failing component test proving EvolveRunner cannot discriminate an over-budget candidate today, confirm it fails for the right reason (missing constructor/API), then add the injectable BenchmarkRunner and benchmarkBudgets request field
    status: completed
  - id: T2
    slice: add :cli's Gradle dependency on :benchmarks and wire --benchmark/--benchmark-budget in EvolveCommand to a real JmhBenchmarkRunner, with a failing-then-passing CLI acceptance test
    status: completed
  - id: T3
    slice: mutation-check both wiring points by reverting each to its prior inert behaviour, confirming the new tests fail for the right reason, then restoring byte-identical
    status: completed
  - id: T4
    slice: update README, docs/wiki/architecture.md and docs/architecture/module-boundaries.md, which all asserted no CLI run could produce benchmark evidence and that :cli had no dependency on :benchmarks
    status: completed

non_goals:
  - making JmhBenchmarkRunner read the candidate's realized diff or recompile candidate-specific code; it still runs a fixed set of JMH benchmark classes compiled ahead of time in :benchmarks, today just WorkflowGraphBenchmark
  - adding further JMH benchmark classes beyond the existing WorkflowGraphBenchmark
  - giving adapters, where EvolveRunner lives, a dependency on :benchmarks; the concrete JmhBenchmarkRunner is composed only in :cli, the composition root, and passed to EvolveRunner as the existing BenchmarkRunner port
  - changing PhenotypeBridgeScorer, PhenotypeFitnessScorer, FitnessScorer or MutationEvaluationLoop; budgetScore already worked correctly once given real evidence, so nothing there needed to change
  - population, ranking or selection across several benchmarked candidates

risks:
  - the only benchmark class that ships, WorkflowGraphBenchmark, is a fixed microbenchmark of SAAA's own domain code rather than of the candidate under evaluation, so cost_latency_budget can now vary but still measures something a caller must choose and interpret deliberately; a badly chosen --benchmark/--benchmark-budget pair is easy to configure meaninglessly
  - JMH runs in-process inside the CLI's own JVM with forks=0, so a benchmark with side effects or excessive iterations could slow or destabilize a saaa-evolve run; this is unchanged from JmhBenchmarkRunner's existing integration-tested behaviour, just newly reachable from the CLI
